3125, 0.0229339599609375, -0.0235137939453125, -0.023223876953125, -0.018463134765625, 0.0189361572265625, -0.0303192138671875, -0.006008148193359375, 0.0604248046875, 0.006931304931640625, 0.01123809814453125, 0.02728271484375, 0.00992584228515625, 0.023345947265625, 0.0191497802734375, -0.0097808837890625, -0.01702880859375, 0.019287109375, 0.0141143798828125, 0.00893402099609375, -0.00385284423828125, -0.005886077880859375, -0.01146697998046875, 0.0103607177734375, 0.002895355224609375, 0.00922393798828125, -0.0010814666748046875, 0.00984954833984375, -0.00803375244140625, 0.012176513671875, -0.0186920166015625, -0.00318145751953125, -0.006458282470703125, 0.0094451904296875, 0.0155181884765625, 0.0082244873046875, 0.004459381103515625, 0.01544189453125, 0.0289459228515625, 0.002002716064453125, 0.035675048828125, -0.0213623046875, 0.01959228515625, 0.0219879150390625, -0.0355224609375, 0.025360107421875, -0.0057525634765625, -0.0016870498657226562, 0.0134124755859375, -0.04248046875, -0.01241302490234375, -0.00958251953125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are pivotal for achieving low-carbon heat supply, but their efficient and reliable operation remains challenging, especially for systems with complex, meshed topologies, nonlinear hydraulic and thermal constraints, time-varying renewable sources, and integrated thermal storage. Traditional optimization approaches\u2014commonly mixed-integer nonlinear programming (MINLP) solvers and local, gradient-based methods\u2014often fail to efficiently address these challenges at scale. While much of the literature centers on steady-state optimization or dynamic simulation, scalable global optimization for large, realistic, multi-period district heating networks remains an open research challenge.\n\n**Objectives and Main Contributions:**  \nThis paper addresses the need for scalable, high-fidelity global optimization of district heating networks. Our main goals are: (i) to develop an improved network modeling framework that preserves both hydraulic and thermal nonlinearities, (ii) to introduce and detail algorithmic enhancements for global optimization tailored to large, meshed networks with storage and multi-period operation, and (iii) to systematically benchmark our methods against established MINLP and local optimization solvers. The principal contributions are as follows:  \n- Quantitative characterization of the steady-state approximation's applicability in district heating, with accessible criteria.\n- Development of specific branch-and-bound and symmetry-exploiting techniques for scalable optimization, with key steps summarized below.\n- Release of reproducible synthetic and real-world test sets, with detailed randomization protocol and full data/code access.\n- In-depth analysis of performance, uncertainties, and limitations, along with practical model selection guidance.\n\n**Modeling Framework and Assumptions:**  \nThe network optimization model employs a stationary, nonlinear formulation for each discrete time step (interpreted as a steady-state snapshot). Core governing equations:\n\n- **Hydraulic Equations:** The Darcy-Weisbach relation is used:\n  \\[\n  \\Delta p_{ij} = f_{ij}(Q_{ij}, T_{ij}) = \\lambda_{ij} \\frac{L_{ij}}{D_{ij}} \\frac{1}{2} \\rho(T_{ij}) \\left( \\frac{Q_{ij}}{A_{ij}} \\right)^2\n  \\]\n  with \\(\\Delta p_{ij}\\) as pipe pressure drop, \\(Q_{ij}\\) as volumetric flow, \\(T_{ij}\\) as average temperature, \\(\\lambda_{ij}\\) friction factor, \\(L_{ij}\\) length, \\(D_{ij}\\) diameter, \\(\\rho(T)\\) temperature-dependent fluid density, and \\(A_{ij}\\) cross-sectional area. Empirical functions for \\(\\rho(T)\\) and heat capacity \\(c_p(T)\\) are cubic-polynomial fits of tabulated water properties, as summarized in Appendix Sec. S1.\n\n- **Thermal Energy Balances:** At each node:\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{\\text{inlets}} \\dot{m}_{\\ell}^{\\text{in}} h(T_{\\ell}^{\\text{in}}) = \\sum_{\\text{outlets}} \\dot{m}_{m}^{\\text{out}} h(T_{m}^{\\text{out}}) + Q_{\\text{demand}, k}\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\dot{m}\\) is mass flow rate, \\(h(T) = c_p(T) \\cdot T\\) is specific enthalpy, and \\(Q_{\\text{demand},k}\\) is the thermal demand at node \\(k\\).  \n  To define unique mixing temperatures at multi-inlet nodes, we enforce enthalpy-weighted temperature blending; the key step is solving  \n  \\[\n  T_{\\text{mix}} = \\frac{\\sum_i \\dot{m}_i T_i}{\\sum_i \\dot{m}_i}\n  \\]\n  if \\(c_p(T)\\) is approximately constant near \\(T_{\\text{mix}}\\).\n\n- **Operational Bounds:** For all time steps, we enforce lower and upper bounds for mass flow rates (\\(\\dot{m}_{\\min}, \\dot{m}_{\\max}\\)), pipe temperatures (\\(T_{\\min}, T_{\\max}\\)), and node pressures. Ranges and units are listed in Table S1.\n\n**Steady-State Assumption\u2014Quantitative Applicability:**  \nHydraulic and thermal settling times can be estimated as \\(\\tau_h = V_{\\text{net}} / \\dot{V}_{\\text{main}}\\) and \\(\\tau_t = L_{\\text{max}} / v_{\\text{flow}}\\). For example, consider \\(V_{\\text{net}} = 20,000\\) m\u00b3 and \\(\\dot{V}_{\\text{main}} = 1\\) m\u00b3/s: \\(\\tau_h = 20,000\\) s (\\(\\approx 5.5\\) h). For \\(L_{\\text{max}} = 5000\\) m and \\(v_{\\text{flow}} = 1\\) m/s, \\(\\tau_t = 5000\\) s (\\(\\approx 1.4\\) h). Based on [3] and industry data, if the simulation time step \\(\\Delta t \\geq 10 \\tau_h\\), steady-state errors are typically \\(<2\\%\\). For small-diameter pipes (\\(D < 50\\) mm) or load variations over \\(\\pm 40\\%\\) per hour, errors may exceed 10%. To assess applicability, users should: (1) calculate \\(\\tau_h\\) and \\(\\tau_t\\) as above; (2) compare \\(\\Delta t\\) to \\(10 \\tau_h\\); (3) check load fluctuation rates. A quick-check example and lookup table of parameter thresholds are summarized in Table S2.\n\n**Algorithmic Methodology:**  \nOur approach seeks to overcome bottlenecks in global optimization for large, nonlinear district heating networks via the following techniques. Motivations and procedural highlights for each are provided to clarify their contributions to scalability and tractability:\n \n1. **Cyclic Flow Exclusion:**  \n   - *Motivation:* Eliminating redundant degrees of freedom in meshed networks reduces search space and avoids infeasible closed-cycle flow assignments.\n   - *Steps:* (a) Use Paton\u2019s algorithm to identify all fundamental cycles in the network graph; (b) For each, select a dependent flow variable and express it via Kirchhoff\u2019s law in terms of remaining variables; (c) Remove one hydraulic variable per cycle. See Algorithm S1 for a complete walkthrough.\n\n2. **Supply-Return Symmetry Exploitation:**  \n   - *Motivation:* Networks often contain identical subnetworks or customer branches; recognizing these symmetries lets us avoid redundant computations during optimization.\n   - *Steps:* (a) Detect isomorphic subnetworks using an automorphism algorithm (e.g., NAUTY [4]), (b) Canonically label nodes/edges, (c) During branch-and-bound, prune symmetric decision branches.\n\n3. **Temperature Mixing Constraint Reduction:**  \n   - *Motivation:* Nodal temperature mixing constraints can be highly redundant, increasing solver burden; removing redundant equations shrinks the constraint set without loss of solution fidelity.\n   - *Steps:* (a) Form the mixing equation matrix for all nodes, (b) Using Gaussian elimination, remove linearly dependent rows (constraints), (c) Retain only essential, independent constraints. See summary in Algorithm S2.\n\nCollectively, these methods reduce branch-and-bound node counts by more than 50% on average (specific results in Table 1 and Appendix Table S3); the conceptual flow is shown in Figure S1.\n\n**Primal Heuristics:**  \n- **Initialization:** To facilitate solver convergence, we first solve a linearized version of the full model, neglecting nonlinear temperature and pressure effects, yielding a feasible initial solution.  \n- **Projection-Based Refinement:** We iteratively refine primal solutions by projecting onto the feasible nonlinear constraint set; this involves computing the constraint Jacobian and moving along the negative cost gradient. These strategies speed up global search by rapidly identifying promising regions of the feasible set.\n\nBranching priorities use the relaxed solution to rank variables by their relative impact on temperature constraint violations and operational cost.\n\nTo clarify abbreviations for all readers:  \n- *MINLP:* Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Programming  \n- *SQP:* Sequential Quadratic Programming (a nonlinear optimization method)  \n- *Bonmin:* An open-source MINLP solver  \n- *NAUTY:* Software package for graph isomorphism/automorphism detection\n\n**Experimental Design, Reproducibility, and Parameter Justification:**  \nWe test our methods on two classes of network datasets, chosen to reflect both typical academic/industry benchmarks and real-world operating conditions.\n\n- **Synthetic Benchmarks:**  \n  100 network instances (20\u201350 nodes, 30\u2013100 edges, up to 4 independent loops) are generated as follows:\n    - Nodal positions sampled uniformly over a 2 km \u00d7 2 km grid, covering typical urban or campus-scale systems [5].\n    - Minimum spanning tree plus 1\u20134 added cycling edges reflects the redundancy present in many European district heating grids [5,6].\n    - Pipe diameters (50\u2013350 mm) and lengths (100\u20132000 m) capture the size range of supply and distribution pipes in typical systems, as reported in [2] and [5].\n    - Each network tested over 10 randomly sampled demand/temperature profiles: supply temperatures [80,110]\u00b0C, return temperatures [35,50]\u00b0C, nodal demands [30,300] kW, in line with Danish and German district heating data ([2],[5]).\n    - Full code, random seeds, and parameter distributions are available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11019271.\n\n- **Real Danish Networks:**  \n  3 anonymized operational networks (32\u201378 nodes, 2"}}